Flatten RuleStructures; drop the Snapshot wrapper

RuleStructures now holds two parallel flat vectors alongside the
before/after index maps:

    pub struct RuleStructures {
        pub structures: Vec<Structure>,
        pub semantic_els: Vec<BTreeMap<TermId, ElId>>,
        pub initial: StructureId,
        pub stmt_before: BTreeMap<StmtId, StructureId>,
        pub stmt_after: BTreeMap<StmtId, StructureId>,
    }

where `StructureId(usize)` indexes both vectors. The invariant
`structures.len() == semantic_els.len()` is maintained by pushing
through `push_blank` and `fork` helpers that touch both.

Walking now threads a `StructureId` instead of cloning Structures by
value. A stmt that mutates state (If/Then, Match scrutinee, branch or
match-case bodies) forks current into a new arena entry and mutates
the fork; stmts that have no effect on current's post-state (Branch's
outer effect) leave it alone. The net effect is that consecutive
statements within a block share the transition snapshot:
`stmt_after[stmt[i]] == stmt_before[stmt[i+1]]`, eliminating the
double clone at each transition.

The Snapshot wrapper is gone. conflict_to_error is a module-level
function that takes `&Structure` and `&BTreeMap<TermId, ElId>`
separately, matching the flat-vector layout.
